INV-linub carried a hand-built exposure table dated 2026-08-06 that ranked
languages by METHOD-KIND SHARE alone and concluded "c/cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell:
0% method-kind -- NOT at risk from this class". WI-lajus falsified that for C a
month later: 156 of 324 corpus socket sites classify as nothing, through
ambiguous names carrying no module slot from #include -- a route needing no
method-kind rows at all. A hand-restated closed set drifts by default, so the
table is now REGENERATED rather than remembered.

The census reads production's own load_catalog (never the YAML -- overlays and
merges make a direct parse a second home for one fact) and splits every shipped
catalogue by route:

  R1  method-kind row              -> needs the RECEIVER'S TYPE
  R2  name in the catalogue's own
      ambiguous_names              -> needs MODULE CONTEXT (F3 refuses it
                                      without, deliberately: str.replace must
                                      not match Path.replace)

A dual row is not a third problem -- receiver typing fixes it, because typing
the receiver is what fills the module slot. The genuinely distinct population is
R2-only: function-kind, ambiguous-named, no receiver to type. All five languages
the old table dismissed are exactly that.

Two guards the prototype needed and this file keeps: both the per-row and the
per-distinct-qualified-name count are named (the prototype reported C's R2 as
16 in one place and 22 in another, and one number would have recreated the
second silent home), and a catalogue declaring no ambiguous_names is
distinguished from one declaring an empty list, with an unknown language raising
rather than returning a well-formed row of zeros.

Tests pin the properties the falsified table lacked: C reads at-risk despite
zero method-kind rows, every dismissed language reads at-risk, kotlin reproduces
the old table's own control, and the census covers every shipped catalogue.

